ment  losses it's not been intentional  underfunding although that has occurred  in these pension systems it's been the  spike in benefits it's promising too  much we have low retirement ages age 54  public safety in most jurisdictions 55  for everyone else that's the retirement  age in government 55 you should be done  you should be in tahoe you should be in  Maui age 55 now I know a lot of  hard-working taxpayers out there who  look at age 65 67 70 and are wondering  whether they can actually afford  retirement at those ages because of the  cost of government because of the  taxation because of the burdens in the  private sector and so there's is there  is an inequity in terms of the benefit  that's offered we see many government  employees retiring earning more in  pension payouts in their first year then  their highest salary working now you  know it's a government idea when you're  paid more not to work then you were  actually paid to work and a lot of these  pension systems have benefits that allow  government employees to earn more in  their first year of pension payouts than  in the highest year of salary we had a  study done in San Diego where I asked  the Mercer actuarial company what does  the average city employee get if they  have 30 year service the answer was the  average city employee 30 year service  not police and fire we're talking about  a blue collar white collar employee will  receive a hundred and twenty-nine  percent of their highest salary for life  129 percent and we have a number of  other abuses in the system for example  in San Diego individuals are allowed to  retire in place and during the last five  years of their employment with the city  they enter a program called drop  deferred retirement option plan where  they receive not only a full salary but  they receive a full pension  simultaneously and these drop pensions  or we call double dippers they are able  to then retire and receive their base  pension  plus a 25-year annuity funded off of the  drop proceeds so you keep peeling back  the onion and you find abuses pensions  are calculated not on the last salary  regular salary but they are calculated  based on all compensation bonus pay add  on pay specialty pay vacation pay sick  pay to spike that pension calculation in  the city of San Diego we had a an  example of innovation where our parking  meters used to have attendance going  around on foot giving tickets and  checking the meters well in past decade  the city decided to get scooters and  these scooters were given to the  employees and the idea was that we would  make this investment make our employees  more efficient they can get around  quicker put those tickets on the cars  quicker and it would be an easier work  environment for them because they're not  walking they actually are just driving a  scooter the union said thank you very  much we appreciate this device but you  know it takes a very talented person to  drive a scooter and as a result we  believe our employees should receive  scooter pay scooter pays about 5% bonus  pay annually now for some of the union's  these people receive scooter paid even  when they're not driving the scooter if  you're on vacation you get scooter pay  on vacation and of course when you  retire you get your scooter pay added to  your final compensation so you know you  can't make this stuff up in the private  sector it just doesn't happen so when  people say well what's the little root  of the pension crisis is that the  investment losses is it that we didn't  pay enough into the system you know  that's happened but the real driver has  been an unsustainable system of benefits  that promises too much that promises a  level of benefit that no taxpayer  receives themselves that are being asked  to foot the bill you don't see this  happening in the private sector of the  nonprofit sector you see it happening in  government and I think really  more than just the financial cost  savings of pension reform 