ink I adding a lot of city  councils  accepted reports from staff projecting  fantastic market returns for their  pension plans that none of them would  accept from their broker with their own  personal investments and and then  they've doubled down on this I mean most  people have not heard of this insidious  thing called a pension obligation bond  this is where the government says oh my  gosh we don't have enough money this  year we're supposed to pay let's say the  city of X is supposed to pay 150 million  this year into the pension fund in order  to have enough years down the road to  pay for benefits we don't have 150  million because we just gave big raises  and we hired a bunch of people and we  started a new program in and etc etc so  I know we'll sell some bonds so we'll  borrow the money to pay for a future  debt so we'll double down on the debt  with with double interest compounded and  this has become a common practice now in  California absolutely and and but again  you see that happening it's a major  problem but the root cause of all of  that continues to be promises that  cannot be sustained benefits that we  would never see in the private sector  because business and it owners the  private sector executives nonprofit  managers know that if they would have  give these benefits away they'd go  bankrupt and they'd probably go to jail  in the / in the government sector you  have seen two things happen to try to  mask the debt one is they use rosy  scenarios on investment returns and  Rosie scenarios on liabilities so they  are assuming eight percent seven and a  half percent guaranteed annual return on  investment every year for 30 years in  each and every year now if you can make  eight percent a year or seven half  percent a year guaranteed every year for  30 years I'm giving you my money right  now and so there are Rosie's scenarios  in the city of San Diego we also have a  policy called surplus earnings now in a  defined benefit plan I don't know how  you could have something called the  surplus earnings because in good years  you should keep your surplus earnings  your investment gains for years where  you're naturally going to have a market  downturn so you a ver Ajay to a stable  rate of return  the city of San Diego we give our  retirees a 13th check to pay out those  surplus earnings so it's a very abusive  system and then of course as you  mentioned they [ __ ] all the upside and  the taxpayers get all the pension  obligation bonds the pension obligation  bonds again is paying one credit card  with another and these came into use in  the 1980s where politicians would say  well not only are we cash poor but I  think we can beat the market you know  we're only going to pay five percent six  percent four percent on the pension  obligation bond because it's a municipal  bond and we believe that we can get  better than eight percent maybe we get  nine percent return this year maybe get  ten percent and so we can actually make  some money and put that money into  paying down our liability and so the  government finance Officers Association  which you know tends to look at things  from a government point of view even  they had to admit this is like a poker  player going to a loan shark to double  down on his last hand no I think it's  actually worse than that it's like me  going to my credit union where I can get  a low-interest personal loan in order to  invest in the stock market because I  believe that I can beat my loan interest  rate with my stock market gains I mean  if that worked who needs capitalism we  could all just be rich just it's just  it's a sweet little turn around and and  it doesn't work you brought up jail and  I thought that this a number of times  when I look this if any private sector  CEO or board of directors made pay  decisions for their employees based on  their what they expected to come in the  coming year and predicted revenues and  expenses for the coming year and failed  so abjec