 benefits directly but we can charge  you 100 percent of your fair  contribution for the benefit amazing  thing happens when you charge someone  the cost of a Cadillac they start asking  about the you go they want a more  affordable model and so that leads to  the second reform which is you may not  be able to force someone out of a vested  contract but you can enter into an  amendment to that contract on a  voluntary basis and so we allow under  our ballot measure people to opt out of  defined benefit plans into 401 k plans  on a go-forward basis why would they  want to do that because they want to be  paying the high contribution rate each  and every year  we mandate and third pensions are based  on a formula you get your years of  service times a multiplier it's three  percent in the city for police and fire  at age 50 two-point-five percent per  year for everyone else at age 55 can't  change that multiplier but it's  multiplied by your last salary or  highest salary what we decided to do was  change what that last salary would be we  said that would only be based on the  base salary no no bonus pays and  specialty pay so the scooter pay  sayonara it's gone and second of all we  froze the assumed increase in pay  government employees under the city of  San Diego model were assumed to receive  a guaranteed five percent increase in  their compensation each and every year  automatic increase in compensation what  I said was what would happen if we  flatlined at zero if you flatline at  zero your liability drops particularly  over a 30-year period over a 15-year  period over a five-year period and your  annual payment therefore drops as well  and the payout for that employee would  be also lower more conservative more  reasonable when they retire so we froze  pensionable pay calculations for five  years combined this ballot measure saves  one to two billion dollars under the  most conservative cost-saving estimates  and it's really a philosophical change  in employee